# CODEOWNERS — the seat map.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES HERE, HONESTLY (see ADR-013):
#
# It routes. Every path below auto-requests review from its owning seat, and the
# ownership is visible on every PR. That part works exactly as it does in a real
# org.
#
# It does NOT, in this repo, force a second human to approve. GitHub does not
# permit a pull request's author to approve their own PR — in an org or out of
# one — so a solo operator cannot satisfy "Require review from Code Owners" no
# matter how the teams are arranged. The starter's setup guide asked for teams
# of one and claimed that made seats real; it did not.
#
# What DOES enforce the seat rules here, with no second human:
#   - the quality gate (G2), a required status check that cannot be bypassed
#   - the two-key check (G9), `.github/workflows/two-key.yml` — a required status
#     check that blocks any PR touching thresholds, baselines, or gate criteria
#     unless the owning seat's disposition and its rationale are recorded in the
#     PR body
#
# Two-key moved from "a review you cannot give yourself" to "an attestation the
# machine checks." One operator, still two deliberate acts, both in the history.
#
# AT SCALE: replace each `@andaro74` with the seat's team (`@meridian/ai-quality`
# and so on) and turn on "Require review from Code Owners". The path list — the
# part that encodes the org chart — does not change. That is the whole scale-up.

# Platform Engineering owns the road and the gate mechanism (not the thresholds)
/platform/                @andaro74
/templates/               @andaro74
/pave/                    @andaro74
/.github/                 @andaro74

# AI Quality owns thresholds, judges, baselines (two-key: see two-key.yml)
/quality/judge/           @andaro74
/quality/verdicts/        @andaro74
/evals/history/           @andaro74
/services/**/evals/       @andaro74
# The eval harness itself, not only its recorded history. `/evals/history/` was
# owned and `/evals/` was not, so `judge.py`, `plan.py` and `run_calibration.py` --
# the code that decides what a published number is -- matched no rule at all.
/evals/                   @andaro74

# Security / Red Team owns the adversarial corpus and guardrail configs
/quality/adversarial/     @andaro74
# **`/platform/gateway/guardrail_config.yaml` used to be here, and that file has
# never existed.** The guardrail policy lives in `platform/infra/lib/gateway-stack.ts`,
# which matched only `/platform/` -- Platform Engineering. So a change to the
# deployed guardrail auto-requested the seat that operates it and not the seat
# that owns it, behind a line that read as though it were covered. Found by the
# Platform seat during the ADR-035 Change A review, by running `ls`.
#
# That is the same fault this file's own comment describes two blocks down and
# `pave/twokey.py` describes about itself: a stated protection stops anyone
# looking for the real one. Third arrival, third seat, third file.
#
# Both handles for the same reason as the block below: CODEOWNERS is
# last-match-wins, so a line naming only Security would REPLACE `/platform/`'s
# ownership rather than adding a second key to it.
/platform/infra/lib/gateway-stack.ts      @andaro74 @andaro74
# ...and what a probe pass MEANS, which is a different thing from the corpus and
# was owned by nobody until M04. `evals/adversarial.py` names this seat in its own
# docstring -- "Owning seat: Security / Red Team" -- and matched only `/evals/`,
# which is AI Quality's. So the module deciding whether a guardrail block counts,
# and the suite that is the only thing able to see it widen, both sat with the
# seat that feels a probe score rather than the seat that defends it. That is G9
# read backwards. `CEDAR_MECHANISMS` lives in the first file and
# `POLICY_MECHANISMS` in `core/audit.py`, whose docstring names this seat too.
#
# **Both handles on each line, and that is the point.** CODEOWNERS is
# last-match-wins: a line naming only Security here would REPLACE `/evals/`'s AI
# Quality ownership rather than adding to it, silently moving the scorer out from
# under the seat that owns the harness instead of putting a second key on it. The
# Platform seat caught that on the diff that introduced these lines. Every handle
# resolves to the same person on a one-operator repo (ADR-001), so nothing changes
# in practice today -- the doctrine changes, and the doctrine is what scales.
/evals/adversarial.py                     @andaro74 @andaro74
/tests/test_adversarial_scoring.py        @andaro74 @andaro74
/platform/gateway/core/audit.py           @andaro74 @andaro74

# ...and the authorization path, which M02 created. Both modules name this seat as
# a co-owner in their docstrings; without these entries a PR relaxing the publish
# interlock, widening GATED_CONSEQUENCES, or loosening the identifier validation
# in the generator would never request it. The Security seat found this itself,
# reviewing the diff that created the decision point.
/platform/gateway/core/cedar.py       @andaro74
/platform/gateway/core/toolplane.py   @andaro74
/platform/policy/                     @andaro74
/platform/gateway/policy/             @andaro74

# The loop is an authorization path too, and in the way that is easiest to miss:
# the plane answers whether a call may happen, and the loop decides whether the
# plane is asked before the tool runs. A change moving the authorize below the
# call would leave every plane test green.
/platform/gateway/core/toolloop.py    @andaro74

# The synth-time assertions this seat pre-registered for the deploy commit. They
# are the only thing standing between "no role outside the gateway may invoke a
# tool" and a template where one quietly does.
/tests/test_tool_plane_iam.py         @andaro74

# Legal / Standards & Practices owns the rules registry
/rules/                   @andaro74

# Tool owners own tool schemas and consequence classes
/platform/registry/       @andaro74
/tools/                   @andaro74

